<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · Lullaby · c. 1884 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Skye Boat Song</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Scottish rowing song of 1884 about Bonnie Prince Charlie escaping over the sea to Skye in a small boat, often sung as a lullaby.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Sir Harold Boulton, 2nd Baronet</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>the air of the Gaelic song Cuachag nan Craobh, by William Ross (c. 1782), as noted by Anne Campbell MacLeod; arranged by Malcolm Lawson</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Scottish</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>London (published); the air heard on Loch Coruisk, Isle of Skye, Scotland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>published 1884; the Gaelic song c. 1782</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · Lullaby</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Sir Harold Boulton, first published in Songs of the North (London, 1884))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-skye-boat-song">The Skye Boat Song</a>&quot; (Roud 3772) is a late 19th-century Scottish song made from a Gaelic song composed around 1782 by William Ross, Cuachag nan Craobh (&quot;The Cuckoo in the Grove&quot;), in which the poet laments to a cuckoo that Lady Marion Ross, whom he loves, is rejecting him. The English words instead tell of the flight of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a>, &quot;Bonnie Prince Charlie&quot;, from Benbecula to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-skye">Isle of Skye</a> as he evaded government soldiers after his defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden">Battle of Culloden</a> in 1746: disguised as a serving maid, he escaped in a small boat with the help of Flora MacDonald.</p><p>Anne Campbell MacLeod (1855–1921) heard the air in the 1870s. According to the folk-music collector Andrew Kuntz, she was being rowed over Loch Coruisk on Skye when the rowers broke into the Gaelic rowing song, and she set down what she remembered of it for a book she was writing with Sir Harold Boulton, who added the words about the prince. Set in the style of an iorram, a Gaelic rowing song, it was first published by Boulton and MacLeod in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1884, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of the North, which went into at least twenty editions; later editions dropped MacLeod's name for &quot;Old Highland rowing measure arranged by Malcolm Lawson&quot;. Other compilers soon took it up. Kuntz observed that it succeeded perhaps too well as modern romantic literature, for people soon began &quot;remembering&quot; that they had learned it as children and that its words were old Gaelic lines. It appears in no older book of Scottish songs.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-louis-stevenson">Robert Louis Stevenson</a>, after hearing the Jacobite song sung by a visitor, probably in 1885, judged its words &quot;unworthy&quot; and wrote new verses &quot;more in harmony with the plaintive tune&quot;, published in 1892. Both texts draw on Jacobite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> nearly a century and a half after the events; Stevenson's version, which gives the boat's course past Mull, Rum and Eigg, seems to describe a flight from the mainland, which is unhistorical, for the prince's only time on Skye followed his crossing from Benbecula.</p><p>From its first recording, by Tom Bryce on 29 April 1899, the song became a standard of Scottish folk and dance musicians, often played as a slow lullaby or waltz in a rocking 6/8 time. Michael Tippett arranged it in 1957 as &quot;Over the Sea to Skye&quot; among his Four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>; Alfred Deller recorded it in 1958 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> in 1959 and 1960, and Tom Jones, Roger Whittaker with Des O'Connor (a UK top-ten duet in 1986), Rod Stewart and Tori Amos followed. Patrick Troughton played it on his recorder as the Second Doctor in Doctor Who in 1968. Bear McCreary adapted it, with Stevenson's poem, as the opening titles of the television series Outlander (2014). Pipers played it as the coffin of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> travelled up the Long Walk to Windsor Castle on 19 September 2022. The line &quot;Over the Sea to Skye&quot; has become a mainstay of the island's tourist trade.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A rowing song sung as if by the boatmen carrying the defeated prince over the sea from Benbecula to Skye, out of reach of the government soldiers searching for him. It is a song of defeat and loyalty, wistful rather than warlike, and its slow swaying rhythm made it a lullaby.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Speed bonnie boat like a bird on the wing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Onward, the sailors cry.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Carry the lad that's born to be king</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Over the sea to Skye.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Loud the winds howl, loud the waves roar,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thunderclaps rend the air,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Baffled, our foes stand by the shore,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Follow they will not dare.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Many's the lad fought on that day,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Well the claymore could wield,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When the night came, silently lay</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dead in Culloden's field.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Though the waves leap, soft shall ye sleep,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ocean's a royal bed.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Rock'd in the deep Flora will keep</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Watch o'er your weary head.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Burned are our homes, exile and death,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Scattered the loyal men.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Yet ere the sword cool in the sheath,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Charlie will come again.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Sir Harold Boulton, Songs of the North (1884). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Skye_Boat_Song" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="l9hOdgI3q2A" data-title="The Skye Boat Song: Paul Robeson, bass-baritone (remastered recording)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Paul Robeson, bass-baritone (remastered recording)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Paul Robeson - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l9hOdgI3q2A" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sing Me a Song of a Lad That Is Gone (Robert Louis Stevenson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1892</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Over the Sea to Skye (Michael Tippett)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1957</span><span class="sg-chain-h">choral arrangement in Four Songs from the British Isles</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Spirit of God Unseen as the Wind</span><span class="sg-chain-h">hymn to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Outlander opening titles (Bear McCreary)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2014</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted with Stevenson's words</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cuachag nan Craobh (William Ross)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1782</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the Gaelic song whose air it uses</span></li></ul></section>
